---
import ProjectLayout from "../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
import Diagram from "../../components/project/diagram.astro";
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="mushroom-classifier"
  label="Coursework"
  description="A desktop app that uses a decision tree to predict whether a mushroom is edible."
  intro="A desktop app that predicts whether a mushroom is edible or poisonous from its physical features. It uses a decision tree, so you can follow why it made each call, and shows the model's accuracy and most important features alongside the result."
  stack={["Python", "scikit-learn", "Pandas", "PyQt5", "Matplotlib"]}
  links={[
    { label: "Code", href: "https://github.com/cgallagher-dev/mushroom-classifier" },
    {
      label: "Demo video",
      href: "https://tusmm-my.sharepoint.com/:v:/g/personal/a00321687_student_tus_ie/IQAGIXd0c7JFS6vCB_qdRPyjAaym1sn9TbgprS5qlBR-dos?nav=eyJyZWZlcnJhbEluZm8iOnsicmVmZXJyYWxBcHAiOiJPbmVEcml2ZUZvckJ1c2luZXNzIiwicmVmZXJyYWxBcHBQbGF0Zm9ybSI6IldlYiIsInJlZmVycmFsTW9kZSI6InZpZXciLCJyZWZlcnJhbFZpZXciOiJNeUZpbGVzTGlua0NvcHkifX0&e=8801nY",
    },
  ]}
>
  <section>
    <h2>Architecture</h2>
    <Diagram
      caption="The window never touches the data directly. It asks the model class for options, predictions and stats."
      steps={[
        {
          label: "Desktop UI (main_window.py)",
          nodes: [
            { title: "Classifier panel", detail: "Dropdowns for each feature and a Classify button" },
            { title: "Dashboard panel", detail: "Accuracy and a Matplotlib chart of top features" },
          ],
        },
        {
          label: "Model (mushroom_model.py)",
          nodes: [
            { title: "Encode", detail: "One-hot encoding with pd.get_dummies" },
            { title: "Train", detail: "75/25 split, DecisionTreeClassifier" },
            { title: "Predict", detail: "Re-encode input to match training columns" },
          ],
        },
        {
          label: "Data",
          nodes: [{ title: "mushroom.csv", detail: "Categorical features, edible or poisonous label" }],
        },
      ]}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Approach</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Encoding.</strong> Every feature (cap shape, colour, gill
        attachment and so on) is categorical, so I one-hot encoded them with
        Pandas and saved the column list for later.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Training.</strong> A 75/25 train/test split and a
        <code>DecisionTreeClassifier</code>, scored on accuracy against the
        held-out set.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Prediction.</strong> The user's choices are encoded the same
        way and reindexed to the saved columns, so a single mushroom lines up
        with what the model was trained on.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why a decision tree</h2>
    <p>
      When the cost of a wrong answer is someone getting poisoned, you want to
      be able to see why the model said "edible". A decision tree gives a
      clear path for each prediction, and its feature importances feed the
      chart on the dashboard.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Core of the model</h2>
    <pre
      class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-5 font-mono text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg"><code>{`X_encoded = pd.get_dummies(X)
self.encoded_columns = X_encoded.columns.tolist()

X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(
    X_encoded, y, random_state=42, test_size=0.25
)
self.tree = DecisionTreeClassifier()
self.tree.fit(X_train, y_train)

# Single prediction: encode, then align to the training columns
input_encoded = pd.get_dummies(pd.DataFrame([user_input]))
input_reindexed = input_encoded.reindex(columns=self.encoded_columns, fill_value=0)
prediction = self.tree.predict(input_reindexed)`}</code></pre>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>
